id: rust.rwlock
kind: rust
title: RwLock
aliases: rwlock, read write lock
tags: concurrency, sync
questions:
  - when is RwLock better than Mutex?
invariant: RwLock allows many readers or one writer, so it wins only when reads dominate and the read path is non-trivial.
answer_30s: RwLock lets many readers proceed in parallel while serializing writers, so it pays off on read-mostly hot paths and loses on write-heavy or trivially short sections.
answer_2m: |
  The cost model is the answer: an RwLock has more bookkeeping than a mutex,
  and with many concurrent readers the lock's own cache line becomes the
  bottleneck. Most request paths are fine with a Mutex because the critical
  section is nanoseconds; RwLock matters for large read-mostly structures such
  as a routing table or a config snapshot. Name the semantics too: std has no
  guard upgrade (read to write), and tokio::sync::RwLock is not fair, so a
  write-heavy mix can starve writers.
when_to_use:
  - read-mostly shared state with non-trivial read work
avoid_when:
  - write-heavy workloads or tiny critical sections (use Mutex)
minimal_example: |
  use std::sync::RwLock;

  let table = RwLock::new(std::collections::HashMap::<String, String>::new());
  let len = table.read().unwrap().len();          // many readers
  table.write().unwrap().insert("k".into(), "v".into()); // one writer
  let _ = len;
failure_modes:
  - assuming RwLock removes contention when the lock line itself is hot
  - expecting to upgrade a read guard to a write guard (deadlock in std)
related:
  - rust.mutex
  - rust.arc
  - design.shared-state
sources:
  - https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/sync/struct.RwLock.html
